<BaseLayout title="Latino Family Center | Community Learning Resources" description="A collaborative community-program project developing reusable art and STEM educational materials and organizing a supply drive for the Latino Family Center.">
<article class="lfc">
  <header class="intro"><div class="container">
    <a class="back" href="/selected-projects/">← Selected Projects</a>
    <div class="eyebrow">Community partnership / 2019</div>
    <h1>Learning resources,<br/><em>built to last.</em></h1>
    <p class="lead">Developing reusable educational materials and organizing a supply drive for the Latino Family Center's children's programming.</p>
    <div class="context"><span>Latino Family Center · YWCA High Point</span><span>Art curriculum · Resource coordination · Service learning</span></div>
  </div></header>
  <section class="summary"><div class="container lead-grid"><div><div class="eyebrow">The project</div><h2>Practical support for a recurring program.</h2></div><div><p>The Latino Family Center supported families through educational and community services, including English-language classes. Children's art and STEM activities ran alongside that programming, creating a need for accessible activities and reliable materials.</p><p>Working with a fellow student through a university service-learning partnership, I helped turn a broad improvement proposal into concrete resources that staff and volunteers could continue using after our semester ended.</p></div></div></section>
  <section class="process"><div class="container"><div class="eyebrow">From proposal to delivery</div><div class="steps"><div><small>01 / Listen</small><h3>Start with possibilities.</h3><p>Our initial proposal explored organizing supplies, using existing facilities, building partnerships, and introducing more consistent activities. Conversations with center staff helped us focus on what could realistically be completed and maintained.</p></div><div><small>02 / Design</small><h3>Make activities reusable.</h3><p>We developed two complementary binders: a monthly art curriculum that I prepared, and a topic-organized STEM curriculum created by my partner, who had previous experience with the center.</p></div><div><small>03 / Resource</small><h3>Supply the program.</h3><p>I organized the four-week donation drive—from advertising and collection to counting and distribution—so the center would have materials to put the activities into practice.</p></div></div></div></section>
  <section class="evidence"><div class="container"><div class="eyebrow">What we delivered</div><h2>Materials designed for repeat use.</h2><div class="metrics"><div><strong>2</strong><span>Complementary curriculum binders</span></div><div><strong>520+</strong><span>Donated supplies documented in the original poster</span></div><div><strong>4 weeks</strong><span>Supply-drive coordination</span></div></div><div class="deliverables"><div><h3>My contributions</h3><p>Developed and organized the art curriculum, advertised and coordinated the drive, collected and counted donations, distributed supplies to the center, and prepared and presented the final project poster.</p></div><div><h3>Collaborative work</h3><p>My partner led the STEM curriculum and coordinated related programming, drawing on a STEM background and an existing relationship with the LFC. We refined the overall project together based on staff input and weekly volunteering.</p></div></div><p class="note">The original project poster reports more than 520 items. The working donation log notes at least 529; the public figure is intentionally rounded down to the independently documented threshold. Long-term usage of the materials was not formally evaluated.</p></div></section>
  <section class="reflection"><div class="container lead-grid"><div><div class="eyebrow">Reflection</div><h2>A deliverable is not the same as a sustainable program.</h2></div><div><p>The binders made activities easier to reuse, but physical pages could be misplaced or damaged. A successful supply drive addressed immediate needs without solving ongoing replenishment or funding constraints.</p><p>That tension became the most enduring lesson of the project: useful program design needs to account for the people, resources, and organizational capacity required to keep it working.</p><p class="source">Project basis: 2019 proposal, staff meeting notes, progress report, curriculum instructions, supply log, final presentation, and personal reflection. The work was completed collaboratively; individual responsibilities are distinguished above.</p></div></div></section>
  <nav class="end"><div class="container"><a href="/selected-projects/">← Back to Selected Projects</a><a href="/research-writing/">Explore Research &amp; Writing →</a></div></nav>
</article>
